hadiths[11]{hadithnumber,text}:
"17724","Yala informed us, Muhammad, meaning Ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Malik ibn Jushum, from his father, from his uncle Suraqa ibn Jushum who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about stray camels coming to my water basins, is there reward for me in watering them. He said: Yes, in every moist liver there is reward."
"17725","Waki narrated to us, Misar narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysara, from Tawus, from Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Jushum who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood preaching in the valley and said: The Umra has entered into the Hajj until the Day of Resurrection."
"17726","Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Dawud, meaning Ibn Yazid, narrated to us who said: I heard Abd al-Malik al-Zarrad say: I heard al-Nazzal ibn Sabra, the companion of Ali, say: I heard Suraqa say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Umra has entered into the Hajj until the Day of Resurrection. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed qiran in the Farewell Hajj."
"17727","Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Malik ibn Jushum, from his father, from his uncle Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Jushum who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about stray camels coming to my water basins which I had plastered for my camels, is there reward for me concerning what I water them. He said: Yes, in every moist liver there is reward."
"17728","Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, Musa ibn Ali narrated to us who said: I heard my father say: It reached me from Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Jushum al-Mudliji that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Suraqa, shall I not tell you of the people of Paradise and the people of the Fire. He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: As for the people of the Fire, every coarse, rude, arrogant one. And as for the people of Paradise, the weak, the overpowered."
"17729","Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, Musa ibn Ali narrated to us who said: I heard my father say: It reached me from Suraqa ibn Malik who says that he reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Suraqa, shall I not guide you to the greatest charity, or to among the greatest charity. He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Your daughter returned to you, with no earner for her besides you."
"17730","Yaqub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Salih, and Ibn Shihab reported that Abd al-Rahman ibn Malik informed him that his father informed him that Suraqa ibn Jushum entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his illness in which he died. He said: I kept asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until I do not remember what I was asking him about. He said: And among what I asked him about was that I said: O Messenger of Allah, stray camels come to my water basins while I have filled them with water for my camels, so is there reward for me in watering them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, in the watering of every moist liver there is reward, for Allah, Almighty and Majestic."
"17731","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwa ibn al-Zubayr, from Suraqa ibn Malik, that he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his illness and said: Tell me, stray camels come to the trough of my camels, is there reward for me in watering them. He said: Yes, in the moist liver there is reward."
"17732","Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysara, from Tawus, from Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Jushum, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me, this Umra of ours, is it for this year of ours or forever. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather, forever."
"17733","Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Shuba narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik who said: I heard Tawus reporting from Suraqa ibn Jushum al-Kinani, and he had not heard it from him, thus it is in the hadith, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, this Umra of ours, is it for this year of ours or forever. He said: Forever."
"17734","Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri. Al-Zuhri said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Malik al-Mudliji informed me, and he is the son of the brother of Suraqa ibn Malik ibn Jushum, that his father informed him that he heard Suraqa say: Messengers of the disbelievers of Quraysh came to us, offering for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and for Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, the blood money of each one of them for whoever kills them or captures them. While I was sitting in a gathering among the gatherings of my people Banu Mudlij, a man among them came until he stood over us and said: O Suraqa, just now I saw dark figures by the coast, I think they are Muhammad and his companions. Suraqa said: I knew that they were they, so I said: They are not they, but you saw so-and-so and so-and-so who set out just now. He said: Then I remained in the gathering for a while until I stood and entered my house, and I ordered my slave girl to bring out my horse for me, while it was behind a hillock, and to hold it for me. And I took my spear and went out with it from the back of the house, and I dragged my spear on the ground and lowered its upper end, until I came to my horse and mounted it. I urged it to trot with me until I saw their two dark figures. When I drew near them where the voice could make them hear, my horse stumbled with me and I fell from it. I stood and reached with my hands to my quiver and took out the divining arrows and cast lots with them whether I should harm them or not. There came out what I dislike, that I should not harm them. So I mounted my horse and disobeyed the arrows, and urged it to trot with me until when I drew near them my horse stumbled with me and I fell from it. I stood and reached with my hands to my quiver and took out the arrows and cast lots with them, and there came out what I dislike, that I should not harm them. So I disobeyed the arrows and mounted my horse, and urged it to trot with me until when I heard the recitation of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while he does not turn, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, turns often, the forelegs of my horse sank into the earth until they reached the knees, and I fell from it. I urged it and it rose, and it could hardly take out its forelegs. When it stood upright, suddenly there was dust rising in the sky like smoke. Mamar said: I said to Abu Amr ibn al-Ala: What is al-uthan. He was silent for a while, then said: It is smoke without fire. Al-Zuhri said in his hadith: So I cast lots with the arrows, and there came out what I dislike, that I should not harm them. So I called to them with safety, and they stopped. I mounted my horse until I came to them, and it occurred to me, when I met what I met of being held back from them, that the affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) will surely prevail. So I said to him: Your people have offered blood money for you. And I told them of reports of their journey and what the people intend by them. And I offered them provisions and goods, but they took nothing from me, and they asked me nothing except that I conceal from us. So I asked him to write for me a document of truce by which I would be safe. So he ordered Amir ibn Fuhayra, and he wrote for me on a piece of leather. Then he went on."
